CAT = "s6Offerings"; SEG = "OFF"
Q = [
('Under Rule 15c2-8, the managing underwriter must make preliminary prospectus copies available to:',
 'Each firm in the distribution', ['Only the lead manager\'s reps', 'Only buyers of 1,000 shares', 'The SEC staff alone'],
 'Rule 15c2-8 requires the managing underwriter to take reasonable steps so that every broker or dealer participating in the distribution gets enough copies of the preliminary prospectus.'),
('Before a registration statement is effective, a rep may:',
 'Take indications of interest', ['Accept payment for shares', 'Send trade confirmations', 'Promise a set allocation'],
 'Indications of interest are non-binding, so they are allowed in the cooling-off period. Section 5 bars sales, and therefore payments and confirmations, until the registration is effective.'),
('A red herring usually lacks which item found in the final prospectus?',
 'Final offering price', ['Business description', 'Material risk factors', 'Planned use of proceeds'],
 'The preliminary prospectus describes the business, risks and use of proceeds, but the final price and spread are set only when the registration becomes effective.'),
('Underwriters hold a due diligence meeting mainly to:',
 'Check the registration data', ['Set each buyer\'s allocation', 'Stabilize the share price', 'Pay the selling group'],
 'At the due diligence meeting the underwriters and issuer review the accuracy and completeness of the registration statement and prospectus before the offering.'),
('Selling group members differ from syndicate members because they:',
 'Carry no underwriting liability', ['Set the public offering price', 'Sign the registration statement', 'Buy shares back from investors'],
 'Selling group firms help distribute the issue as agents under a selling group agreement and do not commit capital to the underwriting, unlike syndicate members.'),
('Registering a new issue under each state\'s securities law is called:',
 'Blue-skying', ['Shelf registration', 'Stabilizing', 'Due diligence'],
 'State securities laws are called blue-sky laws, so registering an offering in the states where it will be sold is blue-skying the issue.'),
('Under SEC Rule 15c2-8, for an issuer\'s first registered offering, a preliminary prospectus goes to buyers before confirmations by at least:',
 '48 hours', ['24 hours', '5 business days', '10 days'],
 'Rule 15c2-8(b) requires the preliminary prospectus to be sent at least 48 hours before confirmations when the issuer has not been a reporting company.'),
('Antifraud rules in Section 17 of the Securities Act cover:',
 'Exempt and registered securities', ['Only offerings registered with SEC', 'Only exchange-listed stock trades', 'Only trades by insiders'],
 'Section 17 applies to the offer or sale of any security, including exempt securities such as municipal bonds. Exemption from registration is never exemption from fraud rules.'),
('Which product must be registered under the Securities Act before it is sold?',
 'Variable annuity', ['Municipal bond', 'Treasury note', 'Fixed annuity'],
 'A variable annuity is a security because the owner bears investment risk, so it is registered and sold by prospectus. Municipal and Treasury issues are exempt, and fixed annuities are insurance, not securities.'),
('Under Section 12 of the Securities Act, a buyer sold a nonexempt, unregistered security may:',
 'Recover the purchase price', ['Only file a complaint', 'Claim triple damages at once', 'Keep it and pay half'],
 'Section 12 gives the buyer a civil right to rescind, recovering the price paid plus interest, less income received, when a security was sold in violation of Section 5.'),
('Section 5 of the Securities Act prohibits:',
 'Selling before registration', ['Trading listed stock on margin', 'Selling funds in bank lobbies', 'Holding shares in street name'],
 'Section 5 bars selling a nonexempt security through interstate commerce until its registration statement is effective, and requires a prospectus with the sale.'),
('Under Rule 501, which investor is accredited because of position, whatever their wealth?',
 'Director of the issuer', ['First-time retail buyer', 'Beneficiary of a trust', 'Large donor to an endowment'],
 'Rule 501(a) includes the issuer\'s directors, executive officers and general partners as accredited investors without any income or net worth test.'),
('In a Rule 506(b) offering, each non-accredited purchaser must:',
 'Be sophisticated, alone or with a representative', ['Have at least $1 million net worth', 'Live in the issuer\'s home state', 'Buy at least $25,000 of shares'],
 'Rule 506(b)(2)(ii) requires each non-accredited purchaser, alone or with a purchaser representative, to have enough knowledge and experience in financial matters to evaluate the investment.'),
('Unlike Rule 506(b), Rule 506(c) allows general solicitation if:',
 'Buyers are verified accredited', ['It raises under $1 million total', 'Buyers live in one state', 'The SEC approves the ads'],
 'Rule 506(c) permits general solicitation when all purchasers are accredited and the issuer takes reasonable steps to verify that status.'),
('Under Rule 503, an issuer files Form D with the SEC no later than:',
 '15 days after first sale', ['30 days after the first sale', '48 hours before any offer', '90 days after closing'],
 'Rule 503 requires the Form D notice within 15 calendar days after the first sale of securities in a Regulation D offering.'),
('Securities bought in a Rule 506 private placement are generally:',
 'Restricted securities', ['Freely tradable at once', 'Outside all fraud rules', 'Listed on an exchange'],
 'Regulation D sales are not registered, so the securities are restricted and can be resold only under a registration or an exemption such as Rule 144.'),
('Unlike Rule 147, intrastate Rule 147A allows the issuer to:',
 'Be incorporated in another state', ['Sell to buyers in any state', 'Skip all antifraud rules', 'Resell shares out of state at once'],
 'Rule 147A drops the in-state incorporation requirement and lets offers reach out-of-state people, for example online, but sales must still be made only to in-state residents.'),
('Under Rule 147, for six months after the sale, intrastate shares may be resold only to:',
 'In-state residents', ['Accredited investors', 'The issuer\'s directors', 'Any U.S. buyer'],
 'Rule 147 limits resales during the first six months to residents of the same state, so the offering does not leak to out-of-state investors.'),
('For a new municipal bond issue, the disclosure document similar to a prospectus is the:',
 'Official statement', ['Notice of competitive sale', 'Approving legal opinion', 'Bond counsel\'s opinion'],
 'Municipal issues are exempt from SEC registration, and investors receive an official statement instead, often first in preliminary form.'),
('A municipal issuer seeking competitive bids from underwriters publishes a:',
 'Notice of sale', ['Official statement', 'Tombstone ad', 'Red herring'],
 'The notice of sale invites competitive bids and sets the terms, date and conditions of bidding for the new issue.'),
('Under Rule 15c2-12, a municipal underwriter must get the issuer\'s promise to provide:',
 'Continuing disclosure', ['A bond guarantee', 'Daily price quotes to buyers', 'A yearly bond tax opinion'],
 'Rule 15c2-12 bars underwriting most new municipal issues unless the issuer agrees to file annual financial information and notices of material events.'),
('SEC Rule 10b-3 bars brokers and dealers from using:',
 'Manipulative devices', ['Electronic signatures', 'Discretionary orders', 'Omnibus accounts'],
 'Rule 10b-3 is an antifraud rule that prohibits brokers and dealers from using manipulative, deceptive or fraudulent devices in securities transactions.'),
('Under Rule 3160, risk disclosures for an account opened on a bank\'s premises must be given:',
 'In writing and orally', ['Only in a written notice', 'Only orally at the desk', 'Only on request'],
 'Rule 3160 requires written disclosure that the securities are not FDIC insured, not bank deposits and may lose value, and when the account is opened on the bank premises the same points must also be made orally.'),
('Under SEC Rule 134, a tombstone notice of a registered offering may state the:',
 'Issuer and offering price', ['An analyst\'s price target', 'Its expected first-day gain', 'Projected earnings'],
 'Rule 134 allows limited facts, such as the issuer, the security, the price and the underwriters, without the notice being treated as a prospectus.'),
]
